**`src/campaigns/analytics.ts` — Multiple passes over the same `analytics` array**

Inside `analyzePlatform`, the derived `analytics` array is iterated 3–4 separate times via `sum()` calls.

```typescript
// BEFORE — 3 separate reduce passes
const analytics = platformPosts.map(p => p.analytics!);
const totalImpressions = sum(analytics, a => a.impressions);
const totalReach      = sum(analytics, a => a.reach);
const totalEngagements = sum(analytics, a => a.likes + a.comments + a.shares + a.saves);
const totalFollowersGained = sum(analytics, a => a.followers);

// AFTER — single pass
let totalImpressions = 0, totalReach = 0, totalEngagements = 0, totalFollowersGained = 0;
for (const p of platformPosts) {
  const a = p.analytics!;
  totalImpressions    += a.impressions;
  totalReach          += a.reach;
  totalEngagements    += a.likes + a.comments + a.shares + a.saves;
  totalFollowersGained += a.followers;
}
```

**`src/campaigns/hashtag-analyzer.ts` — O(n²) `Array.includes()` inside filter loops**

`trending.includes()` and `nicheHashes.includes()` are O(n) checks inside `filter()` callbacks, making the overall complexity O(n²).

```typescript
// BEFORE
const nicheHashes = scored
  .filter(h => h.relevance > 0.7 && h.competition < 0.8 && !trending.includes(h.tag))
  .slice(0, ...)
  .map(h => h.tag);

const discovery = scored
  .filter(h => h.volume > 500_000_000 && !trending.includes(h.tag) && !nicheHashes.includes(h.tag))
  .slice(0, ...)
  .map(h => h.tag);

// AFTER — use Sets for O(1) lookups
const trendingSet = new Set(trending);
const nicheHashes = scored
  .filter(h => h.relevance > 0.7 && h.competition < 0.8 && !trendingSet.has(h.tag))
  .slice(0, max - primary.length - trending.length - 1)
  .map(h => h.tag);

const nicheSet = new Set(nicheHashes);
const discovery = scored
  .filter(h => h.volume > 500_000_000 && !trendingSet.has(h.tag) && !nicheSet.has(h.tag))
  .slice(0, Math.max(0, remaining))
  .map(h => h.tag);
```

**`src/agents/tier2/simulation-agent.ts` — 10,000 object allocations per `runIterations` call**

Each iteration pushes a new object onto `outcomes`. Pre-allocating the array and using index assignment avoids GC pressure from repeated small allocations.

```typescript
// BEFORE
const outcomes: IterationOutcome[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
  outcomes.push({ conversions, revenue, spend, roas });
}

// AFTER — pre-allocate
const outcomes = new Array<IterationOutcome>(iterations);
for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
  const noisy = this.addNoise(modified);
  const conversions = this.simulateConversions(noisy);
  const revenue = conversions * noisy.avgOrderValue * (1 + this.normalRandom(0, 0.1));
  const spend = noisy.dailyBudget * noisy.timeHorizon;
  outcomes[i] = { conversions, revenue, spend, roas: spend > 0 ? revenue / spend : 0 };
}
```

